      "text": "1. If both roots of the quadratic equation \\(x^2 - 63x + k = 0\\) are primes, then let's denote the roots by \\(p\\) and \\(q\\). By Vieta's formulas, we know:\n   \\[\n   p + q = 63 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad pq = k.\n   \\]\n\n2. Since \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are prime numbers, one of the roots must be 2 (the only even prime number). Without loss of generality, let \\(p = 2\\). Then the other root \\(q\\) must be such that:\n   \\[\n   2 + q = 63 \\implies q = 61.\n   \\]\n\n3. We need to verify that both 2 and 61 are indeed prime numbers. Since 61 is a prime number, our solution is valid.\n\n4. Now, we calculate \\(k\\) using the product of the roots:\n   \\[\n   k = pq = 2 \\times 61 = 122.\n   \\]\n\n5. Since we have shown that the quadratic equation has roots 2 and 61, and both 2 and 61 are prime numbers, there is only one possible value for \\(k\\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).\n```python\n# Let's verify the solution using Python\n\n# Given roots are 2 and 61\np = 2\nq = 61\n\n# Check if the roots are prime\ndef is_prime(n):\n    if n <= 1:\n        return False\n    for i in range(2, int(n**0.5) + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return False\n    return True\n\n# Check if the roots are prime\nis_p_prime = is_prime(p)\nis_q_prime = is_prime(q)\n\n# Calculate k\nk = p * q\n\nprint((is_p_prime, is_q_prime, k))\n```",
