      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the number of possible values of \\( k \\) such that the quadratic equation \\( x^2 - 63x + k = 0 \\) has prime roots, we start by using the fact that if \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) are the roots of the quadratic equation, then by Vieta's formulas, we have:\n\\[ p + q = 63 \\]\n\\[ pq = k \\]\nSince \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) are prime numbers, we need to find all pairs of prime numbers that add up to 63. Let's list the prime numbers less than 63 and check their pairs:\n\\[ (2, 61), (3, 60), (5, 58), (7, 56), (11, 52), (13, 50), (17, 46), (19, 44), (23, 40), (29, 34), (31, 32), (37, 26), (41, 22), (43, 20), (47, 16), (53, 10), (59, 4) \\]\nFrom this list, the only pair of prime numbers that sum to 63 is \\( (59, 14) \\), but 14 is not a prime number. Therefore, the only valid pair is \\( (59, 7) \\) or \\( (7, 59) \\), since the order does not matter in the product \\( pq \\).\n\nFor the pair \\( (59, 7) \\):\n\\[ k = 59 \\cdot 7 = 413 \\]\n\nFor the pair \\( (7, 59) \\):\n\\[ k = 7 \\cdot 59 = 413 \\]\n\nThus, the only possible value of \\( k \\) is 413. Therefore, the number of possible values of \\( k \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{1} \\]",
